+  "content": "<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Children must be at least six to enter class one at government secondary schools from the 2027 school year, up from five, under a revised admission policy from the Ministry of Education, <a href=\"https://www.thedailystar.net/news/bangladesh/news/class-one-admission-age-raised-six-4322521\">The Daily Star reported</a> on 13 November.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What changes</h3>\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li><strong>Age:</strong> applicants must be six or older, and under seven, on 1 January 2027, the newspaper reported.</li>\n<li><strong>Why:</strong> the National Education Policy 2010 sets six as the standard entry age, according to the report. A separate policy for private schools, issued on 10 November, already set the minimum at six.</li>\n<li><strong>Special needs:</strong> children with special needs can get an age relaxation of up to five years, and 2% of seats are reserved for them.</li>\n<li><strong>Proof of age:</strong> an attested copy of the online birth registration certificate.</li>\n<li><strong>Selection:</strong> class one places are still filled by digital lottery. Classes two to nine use written tests based on the previous year's textbooks.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Other seat quotas stay: 5% for children of freedom fighters, 3% for siblings, 2% for twins and 2% for children of eligible education ministry staff. In Dhaka city, 40% of seats are kept for children living in set catchment areas. Each section can take up to 55 students.</p>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The policy applies to government secondary schools. The report does not say whether government primary schools, run by the Directorate of Primary Education, will follow the same rule, or how many children are affected.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Why it matters</h3>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Some children who would have started class one in 2027 at five will now wait a year. NGOs that run pre-primary and early learning centres, and those supporting children with disabilities, may see more five-year-olds needing a place. Families without an online birth certificate could struggle to apply, so birth registration support remains important.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What to watch</h3>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The admission lottery timetable for 2027, and whether the Directorate of Primary Education and NGO-run schools adopt the same age rule.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Sources</h3>\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li><a href=\"https://www.thedailystar.net/news/bangladesh/news/class-one-admission-age-raised-six-4322521\">Class one admission age raised to six</a>, The Daily Star, 13 November 2026</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.thedailystar.net/news/bangladesh/education/news/class-one-admission-age-raised-6-govt-schools-2027-4322126\">Class one admission age raised to 6 in govt schools from 2027</a>, The Daily Star, 12 November 2026</li>\n</ul>\n"
+}
diff --git a/src/data/posts/cop31-bangladesh-adaptation-need-8-5-billion-under-half-billion-international.json b/src/data/posts/cop31-bangladesh-adaptation-need-8-5-billion-under-half-billion-international.json
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..6c10ebe
--- /dev/null
+++ b/src/data/posts/cop31-bangladesh-adaptation-need-8-5-billion-under-half-billion-international.json
@@ -0,0 +1,20 @@
+{
+  "slug": "cop31-bangladesh-adaptation-need-8-5-billion-under-half-billion-international",
+  "date": "2026-11-13T21:40:00",
+  "modified": "2026-11-13T21:40:00",
+  "title": "Bangladesh gets under $0.5bn a year from abroad for adaptation",
+  "excerpt": "It needs $8.5 billion a year for adaptation and pays 88% from its own budget, a COP31 side event heard.",
+  "categories": [
+    "news",
+    "funding"
+  ],
+  "tags": [
+    "climate-change",
+    "climate-disaster-resilience",
+    "aid-financing",
+    "gender-equality",
+    "national"
+  ],
+  "image": null,
+  "content": "<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Bangladesh needs about US$8.5 billion a year for climate adaptation but has around $4 billion, and less than $0.5 billion of that comes from international sources, according to figures presented at a COP31 side event in Antalya, Türkiye, on Wednesday, 11 November. The <a href=\"https://www.undp.org/bangladesh/press-releases/grant-based-adaptation-finance-essential-scale-bangladeshs-adaptation-success-globally-state-minister-environment\">United Nations Development Programme (UNDP) released the figures</a> on 12 November.</p>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The event, \"Climate Change Adaptation in Bangladesh: From National Success to Global Scale-Up\", was held at the Bangladesh Pavilion by the Ministry of Environment, Forest and Climate Change and UNDP.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Key figures</h3>\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li><strong>Annual adaptation need:</strong> about $8.5 billion.</li>\n<li><strong>Available now:</strong> around $4 billion a year, of which under $0.5 billion is international.</li>\n<li><strong>Paid at home:</strong> nearly 88% of adaptation spending comes from the national budget.</li>\n<li><strong>Tracking:</strong> climate spending is tracked across 25 ministries and divisions.</li>\n<li><strong>Early warning:</strong> more than 4,200 cyclone shelters and over 76,000 volunteers who carry warnings. A single cyclone in 1991 killed close to 140,000 people.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">All figures are as given in the UNDP press release. Devlens has not seen the underlying government estimate. At a separate delegation briefing on 13 November, officials put Bangladesh's total climate need, not only adaptation, at <a href=\"/2026/11/14/cop31-bangladesh-briefing-climate-needs-11-billion-cif-30-million/\">$10 billion to $11 billion a year</a>.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Who said what</h3>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Shaikh Faridul Islam, State Minister for Environment, Forest and Climate Change, said \"adequate, predictable and grant-based climate finance is essential to scale up Bangladesh's adaptation success\", according to UNDP. Prof S M Abdul-Awal, Principal Coordinator (SDG Affairs) at the Prime Minister's Office, said \"adaptation is development\" and that progress on the Sustainable Development Goals is not secure without it.</p>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Panellists included Ulrika Åkesson of the Swedish International Development Cooperation Agency (Sida) and Dr Daouda Ndiaye of the Islamic Development Bank. The release does not report what they said.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Women and climate finance</h3>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">At a separate Bangladesh Pavilion event on gender-responsive climate finance, held with UN Women Bangladesh, State Minister for Foreign Affairs Shama Obaed Islam said safety, mobility and the participation of women \"must be built into adaptation by design, not added as afterthoughts\". Her remarks were reported by <a href=\"https://www.dailywaadaa.com/foreign-affairs/2026/11/13/bangladesh-seeks-cost-free-climate-finance-for-women-at-cop31\">Daily Waadaa</a> from a Ministry of Foreign Affairs press release, which says the event took place on Thursday.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Why it matters</h3>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The numbers put a figure on the gap behind Bangladesh's call for grants rather than loans. If international money stays under $0.5 billion a year, most adaptation work will keep relying on the national budget and on NGO and community programmes funded project by project. NGOs bidding for adaptation funds may find donors asking for gender and locally led design as standard.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What to watch</h3>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Whether the COP31 cover text sets any baseline or share for adaptation finance, after the plan to triple it was <a href=\"/2026/11/13/cop31-tripling-adaptation-finance-left-off-agenda-african-group/\">kept off the formal agenda</a>. The talks close on 20 November.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Sources</h3>\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li><a href=\"https://www.undp.org/bangladesh/press-releases/grant-based-adaptation-finance-essential-scale-bangladeshs-adaptation-success-globally-state-minister-environment\">Grant-based adaptation finance essential to scale Bangladesh's adaptation success globally: State Minister for Environment</a>, UNDP Bangladesh, 12 November 2026</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.dailywaadaa.com/foreign-affairs/2026/11/13/bangladesh-seeks-cost-free-climate-finance-for-women-at-cop31\">Bangladesh seeks cost-free climate finance for women at COP31</a>, Daily Waadaa, 13 November 2026</li>\n<li><a href=\"/2026/11/12/cop31-pm-tarique-rahman-national-statement-five-proposals-adaptation-50-percent/\">PM asks COP31 to put half of climate finance into adaptation</a>, Devlens, 12 November 2026</li>\n</ul>\n"
+}
diff --git a/src/data/posts/cpd-nazneen-ahmed-late-wage-board-garment-unrest.json b/src/data/posts/cpd-nazneen-ahmed-late-wage-board-garment-unrest.json
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..34c7e90
--- /dev/null
+++ b/src/data/posts/cpd-nazneen-ahmed-late-wage-board-garment-unrest.json
@@ -0,0 +1,19 @@
+{
+  "slug": "cpd-nazneen-ahmed-late-wage-board-garment-unrest",
+  "date": "2026-11-13T21:55:00",
+  "modified": "2026-11-13T21:55:00",
+  "title": "Late wage board fuelled garment unrest, says CPD's Nazneen Ahmed",
+  "excerpt": "CPD's executive director says wages should be set on time, using living-cost data, before protests start.",
+  "categories": [
+    "news",
+    "research"
+  ],
+  "tags": [
+    "garments",
+    "poverty-reduction-decent-employment",
+    "livelihood",
+    "national"
+  ],
+  "image": null,
+  "content": "<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Garment unrest could have been avoided if the minimum wage board had been formed before workers began protesting, Dr Nazneen Ahmed, executive director of the Centre for Policy Dialogue (CPD), said in Dhaka on Friday, 13 November, <a href=\"https://www.tbsnews.net/bangladesh/timely-wage-board-formation-could-have-prevented-garment-sector-unrest-cpd-executive\">The Business Standard reported</a>.</p>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">She was chief guest at a debate organised by Debate for Democracy on whether safe working conditions and social security matter more than wage rises for stability in the garment sector.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What she said</h3>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">A new wage board was due in 2026, three years after the last one in 2023, but the government formed it only after protests began, Dr Ahmed said. \"It is not desirable to take initiatives to increase workers' wages after an unstable situation has emerged,\" she said.</p>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">She said neither side is satisfied. Workers are unhappy with pay and conditions, while factory owners face energy shortages, port problems and low productivity.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Her recommendations</h3>\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li>Set the minimum wage using inflation, living costs and workers' spending on education and health.</li>\n<li>Have the government collect living-cost data from a large share of the population.</li>\n<li>Take factory owners' ability to pay into account.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Hasan Ahmed Chowdhury Kiran, chairman of Debate for Democracy, said the situation had improved after weeks of protests and road blockades over workers' five-point demands, but stability had not fully returned. He warned that the unrest could cost buyers' confidence and exports.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Why it matters</h3>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The wage board has <a href=\"/2026/11/04/new-garment-wages-to-start-in-december-wage-board-agrees-at-first-meeting/\">agreed that new wages will start in December</a>, and owners are <a href=\"/2026/11/12/garment-owners-consider-closing-210-ashulia-factories-section-13/\">considering closing 210 factories in Ashulia</a> under Section 13 of the labour law. Labour rights NGOs, legal aid groups and programmes for garment workers' health and childcare should expect more demand while the wage is negotiated. Brands and donors funding supply-chain work will watch whether wage setting becomes regular and evidence-based, as CPD proposes.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What to watch</h3>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The wage board's next meeting and figures from owners and workers, and whether factories in Ashulia reopen or close under Section 13.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Sources</h3>\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li><a href=\"https://www.tbsnews.net/bangladesh/timely-wage-board-formation-could-have-prevented-garment-sector-unrest-cpd-executive\">Timely wage board formation could have prevented garment sector unrest: CPD executive director</a>, The Business Standard, 13 November 2026</li>\n<li><a href=\"/2026/11/12/garment-owners-consider-closing-210-ashulia-factories-section-13/\">Garment owners weigh shutting 210 Ashulia factories without pay</a>, Devlens, 12 November 2026</li>\n</ul>\n"
+}
diff --git a/src/data/posts/diabetes-seven-in-10-unaware-bdhs-world-diabetes-day.json b/src/data/posts/diabetes-seven-in-10-unaware-bdhs-world-diabetes-day.json
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..0eaf043
--- /dev/null
+++ b/src/data/posts/diabetes-seven-in-10-unaware-bdhs-world-diabetes-day.json
@@ -0,0 +1,19 @@
+{
+  "slug": "diabetes-seven-in-10-unaware-bdhs-world-diabetes-day",
+  "date": "2026-11-14T08:10:00",
+  "modified": "2026-11-14T08:10:00",
+  "title": "Seven in 10 people with diabetes do not know they have it",
+  "excerpt": "Survey data show diabetes rising fastest in cities, and only about one in 20 people with it has the disease under control.",
+  "categories": [
+    "news",
+    "research"
+  ],
+  "tags": [
+    "health",
+    "health-support",
+    "research",
+    "national"
+  ],
+  "image": null,
+  "content": "<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">About seven in 10 people with diabetes in Bangladesh do not know they have it, according to the Bangladesh Demographic and Health Survey (BDHS) 2022, <a href=\"https://www.thedailystar.net/news/bangladesh/news/7-10-diabetes-unaware-condition-4323171\">The Daily Star reported</a> on World Diabetes Day, Saturday, 14 November. The survey, run by the National Institute of Population Research and Training (NIPORT), covered 30,018 households.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Key figures from the survey</h3>\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li><strong>Unaware:</strong> 67% of women and 72% of men with diabetes, up from 59% and 61% in 2017-18.</li>\n<li><strong>Prevalence, aged 18 and over:</strong> 17% of women and 15% of men in 2022, up from 10% and 11% in 2017-18.</li>\n<li><strong>Under control:</strong> only 6% of women and 5% of men with diabetes knew they had it, were taking medicine and had their blood glucose controlled.</li>\n<li><strong>Cities:</strong> 24% of urban women and 21% of urban men had diabetes, against 14% and 13% in rural areas.</li>\n<li><strong>Wealth:</strong> 26% of women and 25% of men in the richest fifth of households, against 11% and 10% in the poorest.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Other estimates</h3>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Estimates differ by source and age group. The International Diabetes Federation (IDF) put the number of adults aged 20 to 79 with diabetes at about 1.39 crore (13.9 million) in 2024, and estimated that 39.1% of them were undiagnosed, <a href=\"https://www.tbsnews.net/bangladesh/health/39-people-unaware-their-diabetes-risk-life-altering-complications-1601746\">The Business Standard reported</a>. The Diabetic Association of Bangladesh (BADAS) said its registered patients rose from 30.61 lakh in 2014-15 to 69.37 lakh in 2024-25.</p>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">A study at Birdem General Hospital, cited by The Business Standard, found that 164 of 311 newly diagnosed type 2 patients (52.7%) already had at least one complication.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What officials said</h3>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Prof Prabath Chandra Biswas, director general of the Directorate General of Health Services (DGHS), said every upazila health complex has a non-communicable disease (NCD) corner that gives diabetes and blood pressure medicine and routine checks, and that hospitals have been asked to use their own funds to avoid medicine shortages there, according to The Daily Star. Dr Md Nurul Islam of DGHS told The Business Standard that a national institute for metabolic and endocrine disease is planned and a site has been chosen.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Why it matters</h3>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Most people with diabetes find out only when complications appear, which pushes up costs for families and the health system. NGOs running community health, primary care and screening programmes can help close the gap, since free glucose and blood pressure tests are already offered at NCD corners but many people do not use them.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What to watch</h3>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Whether DGHS funds the planned expansion of NCD corners, and new national data from the next STEPS or BDHS survey.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Sources</h3>\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li><a href=\"https://www.thedailystar.net/news/bangladesh/news/7-10-diabetes-unaware-condition-4323171\">7 in 10 with diabetes unaware of condition</a>, The Daily Star, 14 November 2026</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.tbsnews.net/bangladesh/health/39-people-unaware-their-diabetes-risk-life-altering-complications-1601746\">39% people unaware of their diabetes, at risk of life-altering complications</a>, The Business Standard, 14 November 2026</li>\n</ul>\n"
+}
diff --git a/src/data/posts/garment-workers-oms-rice-tk-30-kg-66-trucks.json b/src/data/posts/garment-workers-oms-rice-tk-30-kg-66-trucks.json
