@@ -0,0 +1,21 @@
+{
+  "slug": "garment-workers-oms-rice-tk-30-kg-66-trucks",
+  "date": "2026-11-14T08:30:00",
+  "modified": "2026-11-14T08:30:00",
+  "title": "Garment workers can now buy OMS rice at Tk 30 a kilo",
+  "excerpt": "The Food Ministry's special sale runs from 66 trucks in six industrial areas, with a 10 kg monthly limit for each worker.",
+  "categories": [
+    "news",
+    "policy"
+  ],
+  "tags": [
+    "garments",
+    "social-protection",
+    "food-security",
+    "food-distribution",
+    "poverty-reduction-decent-employment",
+    "national"
+  ],
+  "image": null,
+  "content": "<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Garment workers can buy rice at Tk 30 a kilogram under a special Open Market Sale (OMS) programme that started on Friday, 13 November, the Ministry of Food said in a press release <a href=\"https://www.thedailystar.net/news/bangladesh/news/rmg-workers-get-rice-tk-30-kg-4323251\">reported by The Daily Star</a>. Each worker can buy up to 10 kg a month.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">How it works</h3>\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li><strong>Where:</strong> 66 trucks in Dhaka, Savar, Dhamrai, Gazipur, Narayanganj and parts of Chattogram district.</li>\n<li><strong>Trucks by area:</strong> Gazipur 30, Narayanganj 10, Chattogram 10, Savar eight, Dhaka six, Dhamrai two.</li>\n<li><strong>Quantity:</strong> five tonnes a truck, 330 tonnes in all, according to the release as reported.</li>\n<li><strong>Hours:</strong> 10am to 12:30pm and 2:30pm to 5pm, until further notice and while stocks last.</li>\n<li><strong>Who can buy:</strong> workers must show a factory identity card and sign a register.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The sale comes amid weeks of unrest over pay. The minimum wage board, formed this month, has met twice, and the workers' representative has proposed a minimum wage of Tk 32,000 a month. Owners have <a href=\"/2026/11/12/garment-owners-consider-closing-210-ashulia-factories-section-13/\">threatened to close factories</a> if the unrest continues.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Why it matters</h3>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Subsidised rice eases food costs for some of the lowest-paid urban workers while the new wage is negotiated. NGOs working with garment workers on nutrition, childcare and rights can help workers, especially women, learn where and when the trucks sell. The 10 kg monthly limit covers only part of a family's rice needs.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What to watch</h3>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Whether the programme expands to more areas or larger amounts, and the wage board's next meeting.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Sources</h3>\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li><a href=\"https://www.thedailystar.net/news/bangladesh/news/rmg-workers-get-rice-tk-30-kg-4323251\">RMG workers to get rice at Tk 30 a kg</a>, The Daily Star, 14 November 2026</li>\n<li><a href=\"/2026/11/13/cpd-nazneen-ahmed-late-wage-board-garment-unrest/\">Late wage board fuelled garment unrest, says CPD's Nazneen Ahmed</a>, Devlens, 13 November 2026</li>\n</ul>\n"
+}
diff --git a/src/data/posts/government-plans-cancer-centres-divisions-national-molecular-biology-lab.json b/src/data/posts/government-plans-cancer-centres-divisions-national-molecular-biology-lab.json
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..52209c1
--- /dev/null
+++ b/src/data/posts/government-plans-cancer-centres-divisions-national-molecular-biology-lab.json
@@ -0,0 +1,20 @@
+{
+  "slug": "government-plans-cancer-centres-divisions-national-molecular-biology-lab",
+  "date": "2026-11-13T21:50:00",
+  "modified": "2026-11-13T21:50:00",
+  "title": "Government plans cancer centres in every division and a national lab",
+  "excerpt": "No budget or date yet. Bangladesh still has no national cancer registry, and most care is in Dhaka and Chattogram.",
+  "categories": [
+    "news",
+    "policy"
+  ],
+  "tags": [
+    "health",
+    "health-support",
+    "research",
+    "women-empowerment",
+    "national"
+  ],
+  "image": null,
+  "content": "<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The government has begun work on a national molecular biology laboratory for cancer research and plans cancer treatment services in every divisional city, State Minister for Health and Family Welfare Dr M A Muhit said in Dhaka on Friday, 13 November. He spoke at the opening of the sixth Bangladesh Breast Cancer Conference, reported by <a href=\"https://www.prothomalo.com/bangladesh/o81j2ewqch\">Prothom Alo</a> (in Bangla) and <a href=\"https://www.tbsnews.net/bangladesh/health/govt-begins-work-national-molecular-biology-lab-plans-cancer-facilities-divisional\">The Business Standard</a>.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What the minister said</h3>\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li>A national molecular biology laboratory, on which work has started. No cost, site or completion date was given.</li>\n<li>Regional cancer centres in divisional cities, linked to nearby district hospitals for first care and referral, according to Prothom Alo.</li>\n<li>A new cancer institute, modern equipment for cancer institutes, and training for specialists at home and abroad.</li>\n<li>A digital e-health system that would allow a national cancer registry. Prothom Alo reports that Bangladesh has no national cancer registry yet.</li>\n<li>Affordable screening for women in certain age groups to find breast cancer early. The age range was not given.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">\"The later cancer is detected, the greater the complications and the risk of it spreading to other parts of the body,\" Dr Muhit said, according to The Business Standard.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Late diagnosis</h3>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Citing a study, the minister said more than half of breast cancer patients reached hospital late: 22% at stage two to three and 31% at stage four, and 34% saw the disease return or spread. Prothom Alo did not name the study or its sample. Specialised cancer care is now available mainly in Dhaka and Chattogram, while about 80% of people live in villages, he said.</p>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The conference was organised by the Bangladesh Society for Breast Cancer Study and chaired by its president, Prof Kamruzzaman Chowdhury.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">The gap NGOs fill</h3>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Families from outside Dhaka often rent rooms near hospitals for months. <a href=\"https://en.prothomalo.com/bangladesh/i3v9u40i0s\">Prothom Alo English reported</a> on Friday that the Bangladesh Cancer Aid Trust (BANCAT) runs Mosabbir Memorial Alok Nibash in Bashundhara, which gives free rooms, meals, nursing checks, counselling and ambulance transport to 85 patients and carers. The trust says it has supported more than 2,500 people since 2019, funded by individual and corporate donations.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Why it matters</h3>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">No budget or timeline was announced, so these are plans rather than funded projects. A cancer registry and divisional centres would change where patients travel and what support NGOs need to give. Health NGOs running screening, palliative care or patient support should watch for these plans in the health sector programme and the next budget.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What to watch</h3>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Whether the Health Ministry publishes a costed plan, and whether a project for the laboratory or regional centres goes to the Executive Committee of the National Economic Council (ECNEC).</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Sources</h3>\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li><a href=\"https://www.prothomalo.com/bangladesh/o81j2ewqch\">ক্যানসার চিকিৎসায় নতুন ইনস্টিটিউটসহ মলিকুলার বায়োলজি ল্যাব করবে সরকার: স্বাস্থ্য প্রতিমন্ত্রী</a> (Government to set up a new cancer institute and molecular biology lab: state minister), Prothom Alo, 13 November 2026</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.tbsnews.net/bangladesh/health/govt-begins-work-national-molecular-biology-lab-plans-cancer-facilities-divisional\">Govt begins work on national molecular biology lab, plans cancer facilities in divisional cities</a>, The Business Standard, 13 November 2026</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://en.prothomalo.com/bangladesh/i3v9u40i0s\">A lifeline for cancer patients in Dhaka: Free stay, food, care and ambulance services</a>, Prothom Alo English, 13 November 2026</li>\n</ul>\n"
+}
diff --git a/src/data/posts/government-plans-tk-300-crore-animal-vaccine-plant-cut-imports.json b/src/data/posts/government-plans-tk-300-crore-animal-vaccine-plant-cut-imports.json
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..7621b03
--- /dev/null
+++ b/src/data/posts/government-plans-tk-300-crore-animal-vaccine-plant-cut-imports.json
@@ -0,0 +1,20 @@
+{
+  "slug": "government-plans-tk-300-crore-animal-vaccine-plant-cut-imports",
+  "date": "2026-11-14T10:00:00",
+  "modified": "2026-11-14T10:00:00",
+  "title": "Government plans Tk 300 crore plant to make animal vaccines at home",
+  "excerpt": "Bangladesh makes only about 12% of the 260 crore doses its livestock and poultry need each year and imports the rest for about Tk 5,000 crore.",
+  "categories": [
+    "news",
+    "policy"
+  ],
+  "tags": [
+    "agriculture",
+    "livestock",
+    "food-security-agriculture",
+    "livelihood",
+    "national"
+  ],
+  "image": null,
+  "content": "<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The government is preparing to build an animal vaccine plant costing about Tk 300 crore (about US$25 million) to cut imports, <a href=\"https://www.thedailystar.net/news/bangladesh/news/govt-eyes-tk-300cr-plant-cut-animal-vaccine-imports-4322671\">The Daily Star reported</a> on Friday, 13 November, citing Department of Livestock Services (DLS) documents and officials.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">The plan</h3>\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li><strong>First step:</strong> a feasibility study, planned for 1 October 2026 to 31 March 2027, on demand, design, equipment and costs.</li>\n<li><strong>Site:</strong> about 200 acres identified in Gabtali, Bogura.</li>\n<li><strong>Status:</strong> the Ministry of Fisheries and Livestock has given approval in principle, but the project is not yet in the Annual Development Programme (ADP), so no money has been released.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">The gap</h3>\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li><strong>Need:</strong> about 260 crore (2.6 billion) doses a year for some 46.5 crore livestock and poultry, according to DLS data.</li>\n<li><strong>Made at home:</strong> 32 crore doses a year at the Bangladesh Livestock Research Institute (BLRI) in Savar, or 12.31% of the need. A DLS unit in Cumilla makes a small amount more. Both facilities are about 30 years old.</li>\n<li><strong>Imports:</strong> the rest, from 18 countries, at an estimated Tk 5,000 crore a year.</li>\n<li><strong>Foot-and-mouth disease:</strong> demand of about 3.5 crore doses a year against domestic production of 20 lakh to 26 lakh.</li>\n<li><strong>Fowl cholera:</strong> demand of 22.4 crore doses against 1.6 crore made locally.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What officials said</h3>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Md Shahzaman Khan, director general of the DLS, said the plan follows the government's election pledge to reduce import dependence through local production and public-private partnerships, according to the newspaper. Prof Hafiza Khatun, head of animal science at Bangladesh Agricultural University, welcomed the plan but said \"proper management and biosecurity measures are essential to preventing disease outbreaks and improving productivity\", and called for more direct support to farmers.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Why it matters</h3>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Cattle, goats and poultry are a main source of income for poor rural households, and many NGO and microfinance livelihood programmes rely on them. Vaccine shortages and outbreaks can wipe out those assets. NGOs that train community animal health workers or run livestock projects have a stake in whether local supply grows and reaches villages at an affordable price.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What to watch</h3>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Whether the feasibility study gets money in the revised ADP, and when its findings are published.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Sources</h3>\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li><a href=\"https://www.thedailystar.net/news/bangladesh/news/govt-eyes-tk-300cr-plant-cut-animal-vaccine-imports-4322671\">Govt eyes Tk 300cr plant to cut animal vaccine imports</a>, The Daily Star, 13 November 2026</li>\n</ul>\n"
+}
diff --git a/src/data/posts/information-commission-resumes-rti-hearings-november-16-after-two-years.json b/src/data/posts/information-commission-resumes-rti-hearings-november-16-after-two-years.json
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..32fe002
--- /dev/null
+++ b/src/data/posts/information-commission-resumes-rti-hearings-november-16-after-two-years.json
@@ -0,0 +1,20 @@
+{
+  "slug": "information-commission-resumes-rti-hearings-november-16-after-two-years",
+  "date": "2026-11-14T09:40:00",
+  "modified": "2026-11-14T09:40:00",
+  "title": "Information Commission to hear RTI appeals again after two years",
+  "excerpt": "Hearings restart on 16 November with a backlog of more than 900 complaints. The new commission also plans to watch how foreign donations are used.",
+  "categories": [
+    "news",
+    "policy"
+  ],
+  "tags": [
+    "governance",
+    "governance-human-rights",
+    "human-rights",
+    "journalism",
+    "national"
+  ],
+  "image": null,
+  "content": "<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The Information Commission will resume hearing right to information (RTI) complaints and appeals on Monday, 16 November, after being largely inactive for more than two years, Chief Information Commissioner Prof Nahreen Islam Khan said in Dhaka on Thursday, 12 November, <a href=\"https://www.thedailystar.net/news/bangladesh/news/info-commission-resume-appeal-hearings-nov-16-4322511\">The Daily Star reported</a>.</p>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The commission had no members for most of that time. Its commissioners resigned or were removed after the July 2024 uprising, and no new commission was formed until 17 October 2026, <a href=\"https://en.prothomalo.com/bangladesh/fkvv9aozy2\">Prothom Alo reported</a>. Prof Khan, a professor of geography and environment at Jahangirnagar University, is the first woman to hold the post. Journalists SAM Mahfuzur Rahman and Khan Mohammad Abul Hossain are the two information commissioners.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">The backlog</h3>\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li><strong>Complaints waiting:</strong> 917 received while the commission was inactive, according to The Daily Star; Prothom Alo put the figure at about 950.</li>\n<li><strong>Staff:</strong> 22 officers and employees against 76 sanctioned posts, The Daily Star reported. Prothom Alo gave 27 working against 68 approved posts.</li>\n<li><strong>Track record:</strong> 5,804 complaints were filed from 2009 to 2023, and 55.7% were accepted for hearing, according to Prothom Alo. Of 324 complaints accepted in 2023, 42 were against private organisations and the rest against government offices.</li>\n<li><strong>Requests answered:</strong> about 167,000 information requests were made up to December 2024, and applicants got the information in about 95% of cases, Prothom Alo reported.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">The 100-day plan</h3>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Prof Khan presented a 100-day work plan. According to The Daily Star, it includes:</p>\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li>District-level offices from next year.</li>\n<li>RTI fairs at universities and schools. Prothom Alo reported fairs at six universities in December.</li>\n<li>Training for journalists and content creators.</li>\n<li>Monitoring government spending and the use of foreign donations.</li>\n<li>Asking institutions to publish detailed information on their websites.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Commissioner Mahfuzur Rahman urged journalists who face obstacles in investigative reporting to seek the commission's help, and called for the RTI Act to be extended to the private sector, The Daily Star reported. The commission did not explain how it would monitor foreign donations.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Why it matters</h3>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The Right to Information Act 2009 covers NGOs that receive government or foreign funds, as well as public offices. A working commission means NGOs and citizens can again appeal when officials refuse information, but NGOs should also expect more requests about their own projects and funding. The plan to monitor foreign donations adds a second body watching NGO money, alongside the NGO Affairs Bureau.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What to watch</h3>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">How quickly the backlog is cleared after hearings restart on 16 November, how the commission defines its role on foreign donations, and whether the government fills the vacant posts.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Sources</h3>\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li><a href=\"https://www.thedailystar.net/news/bangladesh/news/info-commission-resume-appeal-hearings-nov-16-4322511\">Info Commission to resume appeal hearings on Nov 16</a>, The Daily Star, 13 November 2026</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://en.prothomalo.com/bangladesh/fkvv9aozy2\">Information Commission seeks to regain momentum after 2 years of inactivity</a>, Prothom Alo English, 11 November 2026</li>\n</ul>\n"
+}
diff --git a/src/data/posts/malaysia-migration-cost-capped-tk-1-54-lakh-boesl-zero-cost.json b/src/data/posts/malaysia-migration-cost-capped-tk-1-54-lakh-boesl-zero-cost.json
new file mode 100644
