+++ b/src/data/posts/malaysia-migration-cost-capped-tk-1-54-lakh-boesl-zero-cost.json
@@ -0,0 +1,19 @@
+{
+  "slug": "malaysia-migration-cost-capped-tk-1-54-lakh-boesl-zero-cost",
+  "date": "2026-11-14T08:20:00",
+  "modified": "2026-11-14T08:20:00",
+  "title": "Government caps Malaysia migration cost at Tk 1.54 lakh",
+  "excerpt": "Workers have paid up to Tk 8 lakh before. State agency BOESL has begun picking 112 workers for zero-cost jobs.",
+  "categories": [
+    "news",
+    "policy"
+  ],
+  "tags": [
+    "migration",
+    "migrant-protection-and-support",
+    "anti-trafficking-and-exploitation-prevention",
+    "national"
+  ],
+  "image": null,
+  "content": "<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The government has capped the cost for workers going to Malaysia at Tk 1.54 lakh (about US$1,250), Expatriates' Welfare and Overseas Employment Minister Ariful Haque Chowdhury said at Probashi Kalyan Bhaban in Dhaka on Thursday, 12 November, <a href=\"https://www.thedailystar.net/news/bangladesh/news/malaysia-migration-cost-capped-tk-154-lakh-4322531\">The Daily Star reported</a>. Workers have previously paid Tk 7 lakh to Tk 8 lakh.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What was announced</h3>\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li><strong>Cap:</strong> Tk 1.54 lakh for workers recruited through private agencies. Agencies had suggested Tk 2 lakh to Tk 2.5 lakh, which the minister said the government has not accepted.</li>\n<li><strong>Penalties:</strong> agencies that overcharge face legal action, including action on their licences.</li>\n<li><strong>Checks on jobs:</strong> agencies must give each worker's employer, job, contract length and recruitment arrangements so the government can confirm the job is real.</li>\n<li><strong>Zero-cost route:</strong> the state-run Bangladesh Overseas Employment and Services Limited (BOESL) has begun selecting 112 workers whose costs Malaysian employers will pay. This is the first phase of a plan to send 10,000 workers through BOESL.</li>\n<li><strong>Medical tests:</strong> the fee is fixed at Tk 8,000. Bangladesh objected to some of the 51 proposed medical centres, and a final list was expected within days.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">A nine-member Malaysian team began tests and interviews on 12 November and continues until 15 November. Officials said the first group could leave before December. They also warned that anyone, including BOESL staff, caught taking money from candidates would face legal action, after reports that middlemen were asking for Tk 5 lakh to Tk 6 lakh.</p>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Malaysia stopped hiring Bangladeshi workers on 31 May 2024 over high costs and irregularities. <a href=\"/2026/11/09/malaysia-reopens-recruitment-10000-bangladeshi-workers-medical-fee/\">It reopened recruitment</a> earlier this month.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Why it matters</h3>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Debt from recruitment fees is a main driver of exploitation and forced labour among migrant workers. Migrant rights NGOs and legal aid groups will be key to telling jobseekers the official cap and the zero-cost BOESL route, and to helping workers report agencies or middlemen who charge more.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What to watch</h3>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The final list of approved medical centres, the departure of the first BOESL group, and whether the ministry acts against agencies that charge above the cap.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Sources</h3>\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li><a href=\"https://www.thedailystar.net/news/bangladesh/news/malaysia-migration-cost-capped-tk-154-lakh-4322531\">Malaysia migration cost capped at Tk 1.54 lakh</a>, The Daily Star, 13 November 2026</li>\n</ul>\n"
+}
diff --git a/src/data/posts/plan-international-ceo-reena-ghelani-visits-bangladesh-girls-education.json b/src/data/posts/plan-international-ceo-reena-ghelani-visits-bangladesh-girls-education.json
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..46b6fbe
--- /dev/null
+++ b/src/data/posts/plan-international-ceo-reena-ghelani-visits-bangladesh-girls-education.json
@@ -0,0 +1,21 @@
+{
+  "slug": "plan-international-ceo-reena-ghelani-visits-bangladesh-girls-education",
+  "date": "2026-11-13T22:10:00",
+  "modified": "2026-11-13T22:10:00",
+  "title": "Plan International CEO visits Cox's Bazar, meets education minister",
+  "excerpt": "Reena Ghelani discussed child marriage and girls' schooling with the government. No funding or agreement was announced.",
+  "categories": [
+    "organisations",
+    "news"
+  ],
+  "tags": [
+    "education",
+    "gender-equality",
+    "child-protection",
+    "rohingya-response",
+    "humanitarian-rohingya-response",
+    "coxs-bazar"
+  ],
+  "image": null,
+  "content": "<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><em>From the sector: this story is based on a Plan International press release, edited and attributed by Devlens.</em></p>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Reena Ghelani, chief executive of Plan International, visited Bangladesh on 10 and 11 November and met the State Minister for Primary and Mass Education, Bobby Hajjaj, to discuss girls' education and child marriage, according to a press release <a href=\"https://www.tbsnews.net/economy/corporates/plan-international-ceo-visits-bangladesh-calls-better-education-girls-1601226\">published by The Business Standard</a> on 13 November.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Where she went and who she met</h3>\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li><strong>Cox's Bazar:</strong> Rohingya camps, education and child protection programmes and a community learning centre, where she met children, adolescent girls, young women, parents and volunteer teachers.</li>\n<li><strong>Dhaka:</strong> Ivo Freijsen, UNHCR Representative in Bangladesh; Joris van Bommel, Ambassador of the Netherlands; and Susan Ryle, Australian High Commissioner.</li>\n<li><strong>Ministry meeting:</strong> with Mr Hajjaj and senior officials of the Ministry of Primary and Mass Education. Kabita Bose, Plan International Bangladesh country director, and Ram Kishan, the organisation's Asia Pacific regional director, also took part.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">According to the release, the meeting covered primary education and foundational skills, preventing child marriage, keeping girls in secondary school, safer schools with separate toilets, and climate resilience. Plan International said it was ready to support government priorities and Ms Ghelani expressed interest in joint initiatives. No funding or agreement was announced.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Programmes named</h3>\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li>Steps to a Bright Tomorrow: Education for Adolescents and Youth in Cox's Bazar, run with Jagoroni Chakra Foundation.</li>\n<li>Girl Shine and Champions of Change.</li>\n<li>Girls Shaping Climate Solutions: Her Climate, Her Voice, Her Action, including the Durbar Konnya programme.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The release cites UNHCR data for September 2026: more than 1.2 million Rohingya refugees in Bangladesh, over 1.17 million of them in Cox's Bazar, with women and girls about 51% and children more than half.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Why it matters</h3>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Visits by INGO chief executives often come before decisions on country budgets and new programmes. With Rohingya funding shrinking, education partners in the camps and local NGOs such as Jagoroni Chakra Foundation will want to know whether Plan International keeps or grows its Cox's Bazar work.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What to watch</h3>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Any joint initiative with the Ministry of Primary and Mass Education on child marriage or girls' secondary schooling, and Plan International's funding plans for the Rohingya response in 2027.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Sources</h3>\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li><a href=\"https://www.tbsnews.net/economy/corporates/plan-international-ceo-visits-bangladesh-calls-better-education-girls-1601226\">Plan International CEO visits Bangladesh, calls for better education for girls</a>, The Business Standard (press release), 13 November 2026</li>\n</ul>\n"
+}
diff --git a/src/data/posts/rohingya-food-aid-funded-until-april-2027-rations-malnutrition.json b/src/data/posts/rohingya-food-aid-funded-until-april-2027-rations-malnutrition.json
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..a3d43f0
--- /dev/null
+++ b/src/data/posts/rohingya-food-aid-funded-until-april-2027-rations-malnutrition.json
@@ -0,0 +1,23 @@
+{
+  "slug": "rohingya-food-aid-funded-until-april-2027-rations-malnutrition",
+  "date": "2026-11-14T08:00:00",
+  "modified": "2026-11-14T08:00:00",
+  "title": "Rohingya food aid funded only to April 2027, commissioner warns",
+  "excerpt": "Most refugees now get $7 to $10 a month for food, and acute malnutrition among young children has reached 14.6%.",
+  "categories": [
+    "news",
+    "funding"
+  ],
+  "tags": [
+    "humanitarian-rohingya-response",
+    "rohingya-response",
+    "coxs-bazar",
+    "food-assistance",
+    "food-security",
+    "nutrition",
+    "health-nutrition",
+    "aid-financing"
+  ],
+  "image": null,
+  "content": "<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Funding for food assistance to Rohingya refugees in Bangladesh is secured only until April 2027, Refugee Relief and Repatriation Commissioner Mohammed Mizanur Rahman told <a href=\"https://www.thedailystar.net/news/bangladesh/news/surviving-only-rice-and-lentils-4322666\">The Daily Star</a>, which reported on 13 November that most refugees now receive less than the former $12 a month for food. He warned that further cuts were possible if no new pledges arrive in the next two or three months.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What has changed</h3>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Since 1 April, the World Food Programme (WFP) has replaced a flat monthly food entitlement of $12 a person with three levels based on household make-up and vulnerability, under what it calls a Targeting and Prioritisation Exercise. WFP's Andrey Shirkov gave the newspaper the breakdown:</p>\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li><strong>$12 a person:</strong> 33% of refugees.</li>\n<li><strong>$10 a person:</strong> 50%.</li>\n<li><strong>$7 a person:</strong> 17%.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">WFP said the change was not caused by funding shortages. Its monitoring found 70% of surveyed households had acceptable food consumption, down from 73% in 2025, and it said diet quality remains a concern, especially for women and young children. In September, WFP warned that general food assistance could be interrupted from December 2026 without more funding, the newspaper reported.</p>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Refugees told The Daily Star they now eat mostly rice and lentils. A woman who fled Myanmar in 2017 said her family of five receives $7 a person and can no longer buy vegetables.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Children's nutrition</h3>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The 2026 Nutrition SMART Survey, carried out by Action Against Hunger with UNICEF and WFP funding and presented on 28 October, found among Rohingya children:</p>\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li><strong>Acute malnutrition:</strong> 14.6% of children aged 6 to 59 months, up from 8.6% in 2024 and 13.9% in 2025, according to UNHCR figures cited by the newspaper.</li>\n<li><strong>Stunting:</strong> 42.6%.</li>\n<li><strong>Underweight:</strong> 36.9%.</li>\n<li><strong>Minimum acceptable diet:</strong> only 37.3% of children aged 6 to 23 months.</li>\n<li><strong>No vegetables:</strong> 44.6% had eaten none the previous day.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The survey does not show whether the April changes contributed to the rise. UNICEF's 4 November humanitarian report said 10,400 Rohingya children with severe acute malnutrition were treated from January to September 2026, and 177,100 were screened for wasting.</p>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">UNHCR Bangladesh spokesperson Shari Nijman said the 2026 Joint Response Plan seeks $710.5 million for 1.56 million refugees and host community members, 26% less than the 2025 appeal.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Why it matters</h3>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Food assistance is the largest single item in the Rohingya response. With funding confirmed only to April and acute malnutrition among young children rising for a third year, nutrition, health and protection partners in the camps, many of them national NGOs, face higher caseloads on shrinking budgets. Donors deciding 2027 pledges in the coming weeks will set how far rations fall.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What to watch</h3>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">New donor pledges to WFP before the end of the year, whether WFP confirms or lifts its warning of a December interruption, and planning for the 2027 Joint Response Plan.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Sources</h3>\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li><a href=\"https://www.thedailystar.net/news/bangladesh/news/surviving-only-rice-and-lentils-4322666\">Surviving only on rice and lentils</a>, The Daily Star, 13 November 2026</li>\n<li><a href=\"/2026/10/29/rohingya-response-the-2026-appeal-is-a-quarter-smaller-and-still-underfunded/\">Rohingya response: the 2026 appeal is a quarter smaller and still underfunded</a>, Devlens</li>\n</ul>\n"
+}
