+++ b/src/data/posts/bgmea-no-work-no-pay-section-13-147-factories-wage-board-november-16.json
@@ -0,0 +1,18 @@
+{
+  "slug": "bgmea-no-work-no-pay-section-13-147-factories-wage-board-november-16",
+  "date": "2026-11-15T10:20:00",
+  "modified": "2026-11-15T10:20:00",
+  "title": "Garment owners approve 'no work, no pay' closures from 15 November",
+  "excerpt": "BGMEA says 147 factories with 300,000 workers could not run on Saturday. The wage board meets again on 16 November.",
+  "categories": [
+    "news"
+  ],
+  "tags": [
+    "garments",
+    "poverty-reduction-decent-employment",
+    "livelihood",
+    "national"
+  ],
+  "image": null,
+  "content": "<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Garment factory owners can close units and stop wages under Section 13(1) of the Bangladesh Labour Act from Sunday, 15 November, if unrest continues, the Bangladesh Garment Manufacturers and Exporters Association (BGMEA) decided at an emergency meeting in Dhaka on Saturday, <a href=\"https://www.thedailystar.net/news/bangladesh/news/rmg-owners-may-invoke-no-work-no-pay-today-4324031\">The Daily Star reported</a>.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Key facts</h3>\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li><strong>Meeting:</strong> nearly 1,500 factory owners attended at BGMEA's Uttara office. The knitwear association BKMEA backed the decision.</li>\n<li><strong>Factories hit on 14 November:</strong> 147 employing about 300,000 workers could not operate, BGMEA President Mahmud Hasan Khan said. Of these, 55 had already invoked Section 13(1).</li>\n<li><strong>Ashulia on 15 November:</strong> 59 factories had suspended production, 18 of them under Section 13(1), according to the industrial police.</li>\n<li><strong>Gazipur:</strong> workers blocked the Dhaka-Tangail Highway at Kaliakair for about two and a half hours on Saturday. Police used teargas and sound grenades and detained at least 20 people.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">\"Restoring normalcy is essential,\" Mr Khan said. Leaders of five labour federations urged workers on Friday to return to work so the wage board could do its job. They pointed to recent labour law changes that raised the annual increment from 5% to 9% and shortened the wage review cycle from five years to three.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Why it matters</h3>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Section 13(1) lets owners withhold pay while factories are shut, so hundreds of thousands of workers, most of them women, could lose income in the weeks before the new wage is set. Labour rights NGOs, legal aid groups and organisations running childcare, health and food support for garment workers may see more demand. The government's <a href=\"/2026/11/14/garment-workers-oms-rice-tk-30-kg-66-trucks/\">Tk 30 OMS rice sale</a> for workers started on 13 November.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What to watch</h3>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The wage board's third meeting on 16 November, when owners are due to present their wage proposal against the workers' Tk 32,000, and how many factories close under Section 13(1) this week.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Sources</h3>\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li><a href=\"https://www.thedailystar.net/news/bangladesh/news/rmg-owners-may-invoke-no-work-no-pay-today-4324031\">RMG owners may invoke 'no work, no pay' from today</a>, The Daily Star, 15 November 2026</li>\n<li><a href=\"/2026/11/13/cpd-nazneen-ahmed-late-wage-board-garment-unrest/\">Late wage board fuelled garment unrest, says CPD's Nazneen Ahmed</a>, Devlens, 13 November 2026</li>\n</ul>\n"
+}
diff --git a/src/data/posts/cyber-law-revised-draft-rumour-penalty-cut-five-years-comments-november-25.json b/src/data/posts/cyber-law-revised-draft-rumour-penalty-cut-five-years-comments-november-25.json
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..5da6509
--- /dev/null
+++ b/src/data/posts/cyber-law-revised-draft-rumour-penalty-cut-five-years-comments-november-25.json
@@ -0,0 +1,20 @@
+{
+  "slug": "cyber-law-revised-draft-rumour-penalty-cut-five-years-comments-november-25",
+  "date": "2026-11-15T10:40:00",
+  "modified": "2026-11-15T10:40:00",
+  "title": "Cyber law draft halves rumour penalty; public comments due 25 Nov",
+  "excerpt": "NGOs and rights groups have until 25 November to comment. Critics say blocking powers and broad offences remain.",
+  "categories": [
+    "news",
+    "policy"
+  ],
+  "tags": [
+    "governance",
+    "governance-human-rights",
+    "human-rights",
+    "journalism",
+    "national"
+  ],
+  "image": null,
+  "content": "<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The government has published a revised draft of the Cyber Security (Amendment) Act 2026 that cuts the maximum prison term for spreading rumours or disinformation from 10 years to five, and is taking public comments until 25 November, <a href=\"https://www.thedailystar.net/news/bangladesh/news/lighter-penalties-little-relief-free-speech-4322536\">The Daily Star</a> and <a href=\"https://en.prothomalo.com/bangladesh/mk5afz66fo\">Prothom Alo</a> reported on 13 November. The Information and Communication Technology (ICT) Division put the draft on its website on 10 November, according to The Daily Star.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What the new draft changes</h3>\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li><strong>Rumours and disinformation (Section 26A):</strong> up to five years in prison, a Tk 10 lakh fine, or both, down from 10 years and Tk 40 lakh. The offence now covers deliberately spreading false or distorted information that causes public panic, agitation or social unrest. It stays cognisable and non-bailable.</li>\n<li><strong>Defamation:</strong> up to two years and Tk 5 lakh, following the Penal Code definition, according to Prothom Alo. It is bailable and non-cognisable.</li>\n<li><strong>Belittling or bullying:</strong> up to three years and Tk 5 lakh, cognisable and non-bailable, The Daily Star reported. Prothom Alo said distorted memes and caricatures made to mock someone are included.</li>\n<li><strong>Registered media:</strong> tribunal approval would be needed before an arrest over news, opinion, pictures or cartoons in registered media (Section 46A), and outlets would get 15 days' written notice before proceedings (Section 29).</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What stays</h3>\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li><strong>Blocking content:</strong> agencies could still block or remove content without tribunal approval. People affected must be told the reason within three days and can ask the National Cyber Security Agency to restore it, with a decision due in 15 days.</li>\n<li><strong>Search and arrest:</strong> Sections 33 to 35 on searches, seizures and warrantless action are unchanged.</li>\n<li><strong>Immunity:</strong> members and officials of the Cyber Protection Council would be protected from cases for acts done in good faith, and the council would meet twice a year instead of quarterly, The Daily Star reported.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What critics said</h3>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Dr Iftekharuzzaman, executive director of Transparency International Bangladesh (TIB), told The Daily Star: \"The draft CPA was conceptually faulty to begin with, and now this new draft is even constitutionally flawed.\" He said protections for media alone were discriminatory. Human Rights Support Society (HRSS) asked for clearer definitions, bailable offences, civil remedies for defamation and judicial approval for blocking.</p>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Kamal Ahmed, consulting editor of The Daily Star and former head of the Media Reform Commission, told Prothom Alo that the changes were not enough and that the safeguards should cover ordinary citizens, not only registered media.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Why it matters</h3>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">NGOs, rights groups and community organisations post reports, case studies and criticism online every day. Broad offences such as causing \"public panic\" and blocking without a court order can be used against advocacy and whistleblowing, as the repealed Digital Security Act was. The comment period is a chance for the sector to put its concerns on record before the bill goes to Parliament.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What to watch</h3>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Submissions from NGO networks, journalist bodies and rights groups before 25 November, and whether the ICT Division publishes a further revision before the bill is tabled. Devlens covered the <a href=\"/2026/11/08/cyber-law-draft-revised-defamation-rumour-penalty-stays/\">previous draft</a> on 8 November.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Sources</h3>\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li><a href=\"https://www.thedailystar.net/news/bangladesh/news/lighter-penalties-little-relief-free-speech-4322536\">Lighter penalties, but little relief for free speech</a>, The Daily Star, 13 November 2026</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://en.prothomalo.com/bangladesh/mk5afz66fo\">Cyber Security Act draft revised again: What are the new provisions?</a>, Prothom Alo English, 13 November 2026</li>\n</ul>\n"
+}
diff --git a/src/data/posts/dengue-64-deaths-23880-admissions-first-13-days-november.json b/src/data/posts/dengue-64-deaths-23880-admissions-first-13-days-november.json
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..142212d
--- /dev/null
+++ b/src/data/posts/dengue-64-deaths-23880-admissions-first-13-days-november.json
@@ -0,0 +1,17 @@
+{
+  "slug": "dengue-64-deaths-23880-admissions-first-13-days-november",
+  "date": "2026-11-15T10:00:00",
+  "modified": "2026-11-15T10:00:00",
+  "title": "Dengue killed 64 people in 13 days as experts warn of a hard December",
+  "excerpt": "Nearly 24,000 people were admitted in the first 13 days of November. Doctors report more severe complications this year.",
+  "categories": [
+    "news"
+  ],
+  "tags": [
+    "health",
+    "health-support",
+    "national"
+  ],
+  "image": null,
+  "content": "<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">At least 64 people died of dengue and 23,880 were admitted to hospital in Bangladesh in the first 13 days of November, <a href=\"https://www.thedailystar.net/news/bangladesh/news/no-respite-dengue-winter-approaches-4323206\">The Daily Star reported</a> on 14 November, using figures from the Directorate General of Health Services (DGHS). Experts warned that cases may not fall as fast as usual as winter approaches.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">The year so far, by month</h3>\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li><strong>June:</strong> 13 deaths, 2,907 admissions.</li>\n<li><strong>July:</strong> 36 deaths, 9,206 admissions.</li>\n<li><strong>August:</strong> 43 deaths, 20,536 admissions.</li>\n<li><strong>September:</strong> 148 deaths, 43,774 admissions.</li>\n<li><strong>October:</strong> 174 deaths, about 53,000 admissions.</li>\n<li><strong>1 to 13 November:</strong> at least 64 deaths, 23,880 admissions.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">DGHS had recorded <a href=\"/2026/11/14/dengue-1255-admissions-483-deaths-november-13/\">483 deaths this year</a> by 13 November.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What experts said</h3>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Prof Kabirul Bashar, an entomologist at Jahangirnagar University, said recent rain means the fall in cases this month may be smaller than expected, and that December could see more cases than in past years, the newspaper reported. He called for clearing breeding sites, using larvicide where water cannot be drained, and stronger action against Culex mosquitoes.</p>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Dr HM Nazmul Ahsan, associate professor at Shaheed Suhrawardy Medical College Hospital, said hospitals are still receiving many critically ill patients, with more liver, kidney, lung and heart complications this year. He said this may be linked to dengue virus serotype 3 (DENV-3), but more study is needed. \"Early hospitalisation is our main recommendation, particularly for high-risk patients,\" he said.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Why it matters</h3>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Dengue is running alongside a <a href=\"/2026/11/14/measles-deaths-1304-245562-suspected-cases-november-13/\">measles outbreak that has killed more than 1,300 children</a>, stretching the same hospitals and health workers. NGOs running community health, WASH and urban programmes can help with clean-up of breeding sites and with getting high-risk patients to hospital early.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What to watch</h3>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Whether daily admissions fall below 1,000 by the end of November, and any DGHS study on the DENV-3 serotype.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Sources</h3>\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li><a href=\"https://www.thedailystar.net/news/bangladesh/news/no-respite-dengue-winter-approaches-4323206\">No respite from dengue as winter approaches</a>, The Daily Star, 14 November 2026</li>\n</ul>\n"
+}
